guamp: lean metal2vulkan memory pools (bridge 0040), the MB row (the runtime's memory per process) and a purgeable census

fin50 (live-20261105-204852): Maps grew to a 307 MiB footprint with about 5 MiB of the system's memory left; the GPU's wired objects
went 487 -> 554 -> 620 -> 684 MiB in 64 MiB steps, and nothing came back when Maps' footprint fell to 267 MiB. Every block of the
runtime's pools is a BO the kernel wires as it is made, and the system has no swap and no memory compressor, so a block's free bytes
are memory no process gets back. The pools, as of bridge 0022 (adreno-metal 0016):
- grew to 64 MiB blocks, so one pool's slack could be 64 MiB;
- kept one empty block of any size (64 MiB too) in each pool when it emptied;
- kept room for half of the next block ahead in each of the encodings' four pools (keep_room): a pool whose 64 MiB block was half
  placed got a second 64 MiB block before any placement needed it;
- started every pool at 4 MiB, so keep_room took 16 MiB in every Metal process at its first creation call, widgets and extensions too.

Bridge patch 0040 (crates/metal2vulkan-runtime, memory.rs PoolPolicy), each limit set by the board's NativeMetalEnv (guamp-m2v-env)
without a new addon:
- NMB_POOL_BLOCK_MIB (4-64, default 16): the largest pooled block; a placement over half of it (8 MiB) takes a dedicated block of
  its own size, so a pool's slack is at most 16 MiB;
- NMB_POOL_KEEP_MIB (0-64, default 4): the largest empty block a pool keeps; a larger one is freed as it empties;
- NMB_POOL_ROOM_KIB (0-65536, default 2048): the most room keep_room keeps ahead, not half of the next block;
- NMB_POOL_FIRST_KIB (256-16384, default 1024): a pool's first block (4 MiB before), then doubling as before.
The upstream behaviour is NMB_POOL_BLOCK_MIB=64,NMB_POOL_KEEP_MIB=64,NMB_POOL_ROOM_KIB=65536,NMB_POOL_FIRST_KIB=4096.
Device::memory_census (bytes of pooled blocks in use, placed in them, empty, dedicated, imported; their block counts; all allocated)
and purgeable_census (setPurgeableState volatile and empty requests with their bytes, process-wide: the runtime keeps the state and
purges nothing, so this says what a purging runtime could give back). Host tests: tests::memory (41 pass, two new).

Here: nmb_device_memory_census (NativeMetalObjects.rs/.h, fourteen words) and the telemetry row
"MB <process> p<pooled>/<placed> e<empty> d<dedicated> i<imported> n<blocks by kind> al<allocated> v<n>/<MiB> x<n>/<MiB>" (MiB),
after the L row. Pooled minus placed is the pools' slack; v/x tell whether honouring purgeability would pay.
Applies after 0001-0003, 0009-0022 and 0030; the Objective-C++ is not compiled outside the CI (the bridge crate was type-checked on
the host with the patched runtime).

